Negative zero as an integer. `-0` is legal TOML, and this grammar refuses it
anyway -- a subset may refuse more. It is a second spelling of zero: `-0` and
`0` both project to {"integer": 0}, so two documents differing only in those
bytes would name one regime and compare unequal. The float rule already
refuses `-0.0` on the same ground, inherited from the record's decimal
constructor; integers never reach that constructor, so the rule is stated in
the grammar instead.
